The '''History of Guajicutea (1520–1538)''' covers the period from first contact with the [[Empire of Burawa|Burawi]] colonial expedition in December 1520 through the [[Great Pestilence]] of the 1530s. The era saw the establishment of the first trade relationship with a power outside the mainland network, the introduction of {{w|iron}} tools, {{w|paper}}, and Catholicism to Guajicutea, a population boom driven by imported foodstuffs and economic expansion, the arrival of a second Old World people — the [[Volisanian]]s — in 1527, and the catastrophic {{w|smallpox}} pandemic and subsequent famine that killed approximately 30% of the population by 1538. The crisis produced two rebellions — the Bari Buka (1533) and the Warí Buka (1536) — both crushed with Burawi military support. In 1536, Yabí Tabá's Burawi-educated heir claimed the title of Boqor (king), bypassing the traditional electoral system and establishing the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]] as a Christian vassal state of the Burawi [[Lacashe]] colony. Yabí Tabá's papers from 1535 reveal that he had planned the succession as early as 1523, predicting that the Burawi would eventually conquer the Aruanãyára Empire and that accommodation was the only way to preserve his dynasty and people from a similar fate. The Aruanãyára wars began the following year.

== First contact and the trade gap (1520–1523) ==

[[Guled Badweyn]]'s expedition arrived at Guajicutea on 5 December 1520, assessed the coastal cities, and departed the following day. His report traveled back to the [[Lacashe]] colony on the northern coast of [[Abyala]]. The three-year gap between first contact and the beginning of regular trade reflects the time needed for the report to reach the colony, for trade arrangements to be authorized, and for ships to be provisioned and sent south.

During this gap, the Guaji political elite — the Cacique Má [[Yabí Tabá]], the Warí, and the council of caciques — debated the significance of the encounter. [[Kari Buka]], the only Guaji who had seen the northern mainland firsthand, was likely consulted. The Burawi were the first foreign power to arrive by sea from outside the Capuyaquiran region — unlike the [[Tapuya Confederacy]], whose member states were mainland neighbors reached through established island-hopping routes.

== The trade era (1523–1530) ==

Starting in 1523, the Lacashe colony sent trade vessels to Guajicutea on a rotating basis — regular but not continuous visits, likely timed to the calm sailing season. The Burawi were the first trade partner not connected to the Tapuyic mainland network. The Guaji applied their existing trade infrastructure — designated landing beaches, guest houses, trade chiefs (nitainos), and seasonal timing through the lunar calendar — to the new arrivals.

The first trade ships arrived with a Catholic missionary, Father [[Marcos Gabra]] of the [[Baraka Order]], and a Burawi trade delegation. The chiefs welcomed the visitors and gave them a tour of the island. The influx of luxury goods from the Burawi empire — {{w|silk}} garments among them — created immediate demand among the chiefly class.

=== Economic transformation ===

The Burawi trade introduced goods that could not be produced locally — {{w|iron}} tools, foodstuffs, and {{w|paper}} chief among them. Iron was introduced in late 1523 as translations between Burawi traders and Guaji sailors improved. Iron axes and adzes supplemented the copper and bronze tools that had been available since the 1390s and 1450s respectively. The new tools did not replace existing ones but added a layer of technology.

The volume of Burawi goods entering the economy shifted labor patterns. Iron tools, foodstuffs, and paper arrived in quantities that displaced local production. Demand for mining increased as the Burawi valued gold highly and the Guaji expanded mining operations to meet export demand. Workers moved from agriculture to mining, reducing food production at a time when the population was growing. The early phase of the trade era saw a population boom, driven by improved food supplies from Burawi imports and the economic expansion.

The trade was imbalanced. The Burawi exchanged manufactured goods for gold at rates that heavily favored the Burawi side. The Guaji, who had no experience with iron production or the scale of Burawi manufacturing, accepted deals that undervalued their gold relative to the goods they received. The Burawi recognized this: Governor [[Dawit Wolde]] of Lacashe noted that native peoples were generally open to trade and were taking unfavorable deals for gold.

As Burawi trade grew, the existing Tapuyic trade route through the western Capuyaquiran declined. The Guaji, now supplied with iron tools and foodstuffs from the Burawi, no longer purchased goods from the [[Tapuri Kingdom]] and other Tapuyic states. The shift made the Tapuyic groups unhappy — the Guaji had been a steady trade partner for centuries — and the relationship between the two sides grew colder. Hostilities increased along the western trade routes, where Tapuyic and Guaji interests overlapped. The [[Tapuya Confederacy]] that had succeeded the Tapuyic Empire in 1478 was a looser, less centralized structure, and its member states pursued their own trade interests independently. The Tapuri Kingdom, which controlled the islands and coasts, was the main confederacy member the Guaji had dealt with; as trade dried up, the Tapuri lost revenue and influence.

=== Missionary activity ===

Father Marcos Gabra of the Baraka Order arrived with the first trade ships in 1523. The Baraka Order — a Catholic missionary society from the Burawi empire — sought to introduce Catholicism to the Capuyaquiran peoples. The strategy involved adopting Guaji boys into Burawi households and industries as apprentices and sailor hands. Using translators, the missionaries trained the boys as cultural intermediaries who could help introduce Catholicism to the broader population. On the Burawi side, the boys were observed working in industries and aboard ships.

The practice drew on existing Guaji apprenticeship traditions — families sending children to master households — but redirected the flow toward Burawi households and Catholic instruction rather than Guaji crafts. The boys learned Burawi language, Catholic doctrine, and trade practices. Some returned to their communities as converts and intermediaries; others remained in Burawi service.

=== Paper and the Kari Yabí ===

{{w|paper}} arrived in Guajicutea through Burawi trade during this period. The Guaji had Bikumaku — glyph writing carved in stone and clay — but paper was new: lightweight, portable, and high-capacity. By the 1530s, while [[Kari Buka]] was still alive, the [[Kari Yabí]] — the chronicle of his northern voyage — was transcribed to paper. This allowed the primary source to verify the account directly, making the Kari Yabí one of the best-preserved Guaji records. Bikumaku remained the sacred carving tradition; paper became the medium for secular transcription.

=== Burawi strategic interests ===

Governor Dawit Wolde of Lacashe assessed the Guaji as a potential ally for the Empire of Burawa. He noted that the Guaji held political influence over most of the western Capuyaquiran and had an adversarial relationship with the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] — rooted in the [[Kari Wirá]] and deepened by generations of trade contact that gave the Guaji knowledge of Tapuya culture and vulnerabilities. Dawit assessed that this knowledge could be useful to Burawa in the future.

Burawi colonial policy at this time was to establish small colonies in unoccupied territory on the mainland, initiate trade, spread religion, and capitalize on existing trade networks. The policy was applied cautiously in the Capuyaquiran: trade first, missionary activity alongside it, and political relationships built through hospitality and gift exchange. The Burawi also had aspirations for expanding their empire, but immediate focus remained on the Lacashe colony and troop growth for a possible future conflict with the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]].

=== Diplomatic relations ===

The Guaji leadership became increasingly friendly with the Burawi during the trade era. On 8 March 1528, Governor Dawit Wolde visited Guajicutea for a feast hosted by the Cacique Má Yabí Tabá. The visit marked the high point of Guaji-Burawi relations before the pestilence. The chiefs welcomed the governor with traditional hospitality — food, music, and gift exchange — and the relationship appeared stable and mutually beneficial.

=== The succession arrangement (1523) ===

In 1523, as the first trade ships arrived, Yabí Tabá made a private arrangement with the Burawi. His eldest son would travel to Lacashe to study among the Burawi — to learn their language, their religion, and their ways of rule. Governor Dawit Wolde's journal records the agreement. The arrangement was not publicly known in Guajicutea.

Yabí Tabá had seen Burawi military power firsthand during the trade years. In papers written in 1535, he explained his reasoning: he believed the Burawi were preparing for a major war on the mainland, and that the Aruanãyára Empire would be their target. He predicted that Aruanãyára would fall, and that the Capuyaquiran would follow. By sending his heir to be educated among the Burawi, by embracing their religion and their political model, he hoped to secure his dynasty's survival and his people's place in a Burawi-dominated world before the conquest reached their shores. He chose accommodation over resistance, vassalage over destruction. The Aruanãyára wars began in 1537, two years after his prediction and one year after his death.

The son remained in Lacashe through the early years of the pestilence, receiving a Catholic education and being groomed for rule. When Yabí Tabá died in 1536, the heir returned to Guajicutea not as a candidate for election, but as a king already crowned.

=== Volisanian contact (1527) ===

On 15 April 1527, a [[Volisanian]] {{w|galleon}} commanded by Captain [[Pere Vidal (captain)|Pere Vidal]] attempted to dock on the shores of Guajicutea. Vidal had been navigating around the Capuyaquiran archipelago, traversing the coast from [[Balboa]] toward [[Lacashe]]. The stop at Guajicutea was brief — a trade for foodstuffs to provision the ship for the remainder of its voyage. The Volisanians were the first non-Burawi Old World people the Guaji encountered. Unlike the Burawi, who had arrived through the Lacashe colony with a systematic trade and missionary effort, the Volisanians were passing through — a single ship on a longer route, stopping for supplies.

In December 1530, Volisanian traders attempted to establish a trade envoy from the newly established Balboa colony (1530). The effort came two months before the first wave of the pestilence struck Guajicutea.

== The Great Pestilence (1530s–1538) ==

By the early 1530s, the [[Great Pestilence]] struck the Capuyaquiran region. The pandemic was part of a broader spread of Old World diseases through Adonia as colonial contact increased. The Burawi, coming from a world where these diseases were endemic, carried pathogens to populations with no prior exposure or inherited immunity. The primary disease was {{w|smallpox}}.

The disease spread through the trade ports first — [[Yukubá]], [[Waribá]], and [[Barikú]] — then inland. In February 1531, a mass wave of illness set in. Trade effectively stopped. Mass panic spread through the population.

The Guaji chiefs, seeing the connection between foreign ships and disease, attempted to reduce trade — a quarantine measure to limit contact and spread. Burawi ships came less frequently as the chiefs restricted landing access. The trade reduction had a devastating economic side effect: the Guaji economy had become partially dependent on Burawi trade, and when the trade contracted, craftsmen lost their market, chiefs lost revenue, and the tributary system strained further.

The trade cut also produced a famine. During the trade era, the labor shift from agriculture to mining had left fields fallowed — workers had moved to mining operations to meet Burawi gold demand, and foodstuffs imported from Burawi had supplemented the shortfall. When trade stopped, the imported food supply ceased at the same time the fallowed fields could not be quickly replanted. The initial disease death wave reduced the available labor force further, making agricultural recovery even slower. The famine compounded the death toll through the years that followed, as foodstocks were depleted and agriculture could not be restored quickly enough to feed the surviving population. The famine did not end until 1538, as foodstocks finally began to recover. By that year, approximately 30% of the population was dead — roughly 12,000 from a pre-contact peak of approximately 40,000 — with deaths from famine following and compounding the initial disease wave. The population stabilized around 25,000–28,000 as food production resumed and survivors of the disease developed resistance.

=== Burawi aid and the first Dabra (1532) ===

In February 1532, the Cacique Má Yabí Tabá sent a request for aid to the Burawi, who appeared less affected by the disease than the Guaji. The Burawi missionaries had already begun sending aid — they had dealt with the pestilence at the Lacashe colony and had experience treating the disease among native populations there.

On 16 March 1532, the request produced the first Dabra — a Burawi mission station that served as both a religious and diplomatic outpost, open to the local population. The Dabra established a {{w|hospital|hospice}} on Guajicutea, the first time non-Guaji were permitted to live on the island. Burawi missionaries and healers operated the hospice, treating the sick and distributing medicines.

=== Religious fracture ===

The Burawi presence at the Dabra and the missionary effort alongside it produced a fracture in the Guaji religious order. The Burawi had forbidden Guaji priests from practicing their traditional rites and had communicated to the population that they should follow Catholic practices instead. The bohíques and the priestesses — the keepers of Guaji religion, the calendar, the catacombs, and the spiritual life of the people — saw the Burawi effort as an attempt to erase the Guaji religion. The religious order split between those who accepted the aid and the Catholic presence as necessary, and those who saw it as cultural destruction.

Burial was a particular point of contention. The Guaji had cremated their dead since the unification in 516 CE — elites had their ashes placed in niches (kurubá) in the [[Catacumbas del Abismo]], while commoners had their ashes scattered in sea, river, or earth. The Catholic Church, by contrast, required burial of the dead. The Burawi missionaries pressed for the adoption of Christian burial, but met firm opposition from the Guaji, for whom cremation was not merely a custom but the sacred mechanism by which the spirit traveled from the mountain's fire to the sky. The practice was not abandoned. The Burawi instead maintained a cemetery at the Dabra for their own dead — the first burial ground on the island — while the Guaji continued to cremate.

=== Cremation and the first rebellion (1533) ===

The Guaji cremation policy — established since the unification in 516 CE — helped reduce the spread of disease. By burning the dead rather than burying them, the Guaji destroyed pathogens in the bodies before they could spread further. Guajicutea suffered less from the pestilence than other Capuyaquiran peoples as a result, though the disease still caused significant death and fear.

In 1533, as the disease wave finally subsided, a rebellion broke out. The accumulated fear, the religious fracture, the economic depression, and the presence of foreigners on Guaji land combined to produce a violent response. The rebellion — known as the [[Bari Buka]] ("the storm breaks") — was crushed with the military support of the Burawi, who had a vested interest in maintaining the Guaji chief who had cooperated with their trade and missionary efforts.

The depression bred internal conflicts beyond the rebellion. Some blamed the chiefs for allowing foreign trade in the first place. Others blamed the Burawi. Some blamed the Warí and the priestesses for failing to spiritually protect the people. A trade faction wanted to resume trade to restore the economy; an isolation faction wanted to seal the islands completely. The tributary islands, already resentful after the [[Boraku Revolt]] and the harsher terms imposed, saw the crisis as an opportunity — with Guajicutea weakened, tribute enforcement became increasingly difficult.

=== The succession crisis and the Kingdom of Guajicutea (1536) ===

Yabí Tabá (ruler 39, reigned 1480–1536) died in 1536, the same year the famine finally ended. His death triggered a succession crisis that ended the pre-colonial political order.

Yabí Tabá's heir had been educated in Lacashe since 1523, part of the private arrangement Yabí Tabá had made with Governor Dawit Wolde to secure his dynasty's future. The son had spent over a decade among the Burawi, learning their language, their Catholic faith, and their political traditions. When he returned to Guajicutea after his father's death, he claimed the Burawi royal title Boqor — king — rather than the traditional title of Cacique Má. The distinction was fundamental: a Cacique Má was elected by the village caciques from eligible chiefly families. A Boqor ruled by right of blood and did not need election. By claiming kingship, the heir bypassed the entire traditional electoral system and the authority of the caciques who would have chosen the next ruler.

The claim triggered a civil war. The religious class — the bohíques and priestesses who had already been marginalized by the Burawi mission — and the peasantry rose in defense of the traditional order. The [[Warí Buka]] ("the priestess breaks") was fought between the foreign-backed claimant and the forces of the old Guaji order: the religious establishment, the peasants, and likely some caciques who saw their electoral authority being stripped away.

The Burawi intervened militarily and crushed the uprising. Following its suppression, the [[Kingdom of Guajicutea]] was established in 1536 as a vassal of the Burawi [[Lacashe]] colony. The traditional dual-authority system — Cacique Má (elected secular ruler) and Warí (high priestess, sacred authority) — was abolished. In its place stood a single ruler bearing a foreign title, legitimized by foreign arms and a foreign religion, no longer answerable to the caciques who had elected Guaji rulers for a thousand years.

The treaty establishing the Kingdom of Guajicutea was organized by Governor [[Yohannes Tadesse]] of Lacashe. Tadesse pursued a broader strategy of creating allied Christian kingdoms among the native peoples of the Capuyaquiran, building a network of client states whose knowledge of local geography, trade routes, and military capabilities could support a future Burawi war against the [[Aruanãyára]] Empire on the Abyala mainland. Guajicutea, with its maritime tradition, its long history of trade, and its adversarial relationship with the Tapuya Confederacy, was a strategic prize.

=== Reforms of the new kingdom ===

The new king, [[Petro I of Guajicutea|Boqor Petros I]] of the house Venturan — the baptismal name Yabí Tabá's son had taken in Lacashe — ruled from the inland capital of [[Tabakurí]] and moved quickly to dismantle the old order and replace it with institutions modeled on the Burawi political system.

The religious order was abolished. The bohíques and priestesses, already weakened by the Baraka Order's missionary work and the trauma of the pestilence, were stripped of their institutional authority. The Warí — the office of the high priestess that had stood as the ultimate sacred authority in Guaji society for a thousand years — was dissolved. The Catholic Church became the official religion of the kingdom.

The [[Catacumbas del Abismo]] were sealed. The catacombs that had served as the sacred heart of Guaji religion — the calendar corridor, the storm ledger, the ash niches of the kurubá — were closed. The carving of Bikumaku, the glyph writing that had recorded Guaji history in stone since the 4th century, ceased. The calendar corridor's last entry was the year of the kingdom's founding.

The native lunar calendar was replaced with the Burawi calendar. The system of thirteen months — First Rains, Sprouting, Flying Fish, and the rest — that had governed Guaji timekeeping since 516 CE was abandoned in favor of the Old World solar calendar used by the Burawi. The carved year-marks (bikú) and decadal rings (bikumá) in the catacombs became historical artifacts rather than living records.

Political institutions were formalized. The loose system of elected caciques and councils was replaced with a feudal hierarchy. Loyal chiefs — those who had supported the new king during the Warí Buka or who accepted the new order afterward — were granted dominion over expanded territories called ''bikabá'' ("marked land," from ''bika'' — carved mark, as in Bikumaku — and ''bá'' — place). Their family names were Christianized, adapted from their Guaji lineage names to Burawi forms. These chiefs became vassal lords bearing the title ''bikabári'' ("person of the marked land"), holding their bikabá by grant from the king rather than by election from below.

Communal farming was replaced with feudal land ownership. The traditional system of shared agricultural land, worked communally by villages and families, was abolished. Land became the property of the feudal lords, who granted tenancy to the peasants who worked it. All production paid tribute upward — to the lord, to the king, and through the king to the Burawi. The shift from communal to feudal ownership transformed the relationship between Guaji people and the land they had worked for generations.

